VERDICT: FAIL -- RESTCONF can disable and enable a port, but it cannot add or delete a VLAN.
         PATCH answers 204 and changes nothing.
  Method (the database has no steps): from tb470 eth2, send RESTCONF requests to the DUT at
  https://10.38.215.35/restconf as user manager (privilege 15). The interface under test is
  port1.0.3, cabled to the 4050's port1.0.3. Prove each request at five places: the
  RESTCONF state, the DUT CLI, the DUT running-config, the DUT log and the 4050's link.
  For VLANs, prove each request with `show vlan brief`.
  Expected: a request that succeeds changes the device, and a request that does not change
  the device does not report success.
      disable / enable by PUT, OpenConfig model .... works, every place agrees  (STEP 2)
      disable / enable by PUT, IETF model .......... works, every place agrees  (STEP 2)
      disable by PATCH on the leaf ................. 204, port stays up  (STEP 3)
      disable by PATCH on the list entry ........... 404  (STEP 3)
      add a VLAN, 4 request forms .................. no VLAN created  (STEP 4)
      delete a real VLAN, both models .............. 403 access-denied  (STEP 5)
FAIL CONDITION: STEP 4 and STEP 5. Expected: RESTCONF can add a VLAN and delete a VLAN.
         Seen: no request created a VLAN. Three forms were refused (403, 403, 404). The
         fourth answered 204 but created nothing on the device. Deleting a VLAN made by the
         CLI answered 403 access-denied on both models, for a privilege 15 user. The yang
         library has no model for the VLAN database. So the product has no RESTCONF way to
         add or delete a VLAN.
         Also STEP 3: PATCH on the `enabled` leaf answered 204 No Content and did not shut
         the port. A 204 says the change was made, so the client is told something false.
TRIED:   VLAN add: IETF PUT with a type (403), IETF PUT without a type (204, nothing
         created), IETF POST (403), OpenConfig PUT (404). VLAN delete: IETF DELETE (403),
         OpenConfig DELETE (403). Port disable by PATCH: on the leaf (204, no change), on
         the list entry (404).
CAVEAT:  Persistence across a reboot was not measured: the session did not allow reboots,
         and nothing was written. Only port1.0.3 was disabled and enabled.
